Expressing both sides of this identity using \frac prevents the MathJax reader to understand, so instead of $\tan$ we use $\tan$ inside a \frac monster.
🎸晊
Let $f(y)= \frac{y}{1-y^2}$, and let $P$ and $Q$ be the odd and even power of arguments of $\tan$. $f(y)=f(-y)$ therefore as $Q$ is odd, it is their product and hence can be described by one power $y^k$, so let $Q=b_ky^k$ and $P=c_ky^k$ , where for every $Q$ constant, you can arbitrarily adjust one more power. Then, for $k=2022$, we obtain the identity for $\tan^{2023}x$, and find that
\[\tan^{2023}x = f(b_{2022}\tan^2x)=\frac{b_{2022}\tan^2x}{1-b_{2022}^2\tan^2x}=\frac{a_1\tan x+a_3\tan^3x+a_5\tan^5x+....+a_{2023}\tan^{2023}x}{1-a_2\tan^2x+a_4\tan^4x+...+a_{2022}\tan^{2022}x}\]
so \[a_1=b_{2022},\quad a_2=0,\quad a_3=a_{2023}b_{2022}^3, a_4=0, a_5=2b_{2022}^5,..., a_{2023}=a_3b_{2022}^{2023-1},\]because we adjust the same power terms $a_2,a_4,a_6,...,a_{2022}$ to zero. 
$b_n$ only depends on $n\pmod{4}$ - prove by induction. $b_0=0, b_1=b, b_2=-b^2, b_3=b, b_4=b^2$. $b_4=1$ (since $a_4=-1$). So $b_{4k}=1,b_{4k+1}=b,b_{4k+2}=-b^2,b_{4k+3}=b$. Thus
\begin{align}
a_3=b_{2022}b_{2022}^3&=1\times 1^{505}\times b^{2\cdot 505+3}\\&=b^{1013}\\&=(\tan^2x)(\tan^2x)^{505\times 5}(\tan^2x)^3\\&=(\tan^2x)^{2515}
\end{align}
so $a_{2023}=\boxed{(\tan^2x)^{2515}}$.

